{:fh/id  "FH-CTRL-007"
 :fh/law "A caller rejects a live draft by advancing the reset generation, and the rejection lands even when the caller reasserts a value EQUAL to the one it had: the draft stops being displayed at once and the caller's baseline is shown, while the same reassertion under an UNCHANGED generation leaves the draft alone. Value-equality is provably blind to the rejection; the generation is not."

 :kind :my.ui/buffered-field

 :control [:invoice 42 :amount]

 ;; The accepted baseline, and the generation that established it.
 :baseline "10.00"
 :revision 4

 ;; The user edits. The record is stamped with the generation its render
 ;; displayed, and the control shows the draft.
 :typed                   "bad"
 :displayed-while-editing "bad"
 :record-while-editing    {:reset-key 4 :draft "bad"}

 ;; THE CASE. The caller refuses the draft and stands by what it already
 ;; had — the same string, character for character — and advances the
 ;; generation to say "this is a NEW baseline decision".
 :reasserted-value "10.00"
 :next-revision    5

 ;; The draft is gone from the display immediately, with no event
 ;; dispatched during render and no host node reset.
 :displayed-after-rejection "10.00"

 ;; The superseded record is not erased — it is INELIGIBLE. Keeping the
 ;; stamp is what lets a tool holding the caller's event and the app-db
 ;; name the generation that orphaned it; the next edit replaces it.
 :stale-record-after-rejection {:reset-key 4 :draft "bad"}

 ;; The counter-case, and the reason the law above is about the
 ;; GENERATION rather than about any change at all: the caller moves the
 ;; VALUE while holding the generation, which is an external update and
 ;; not a rejection, so the live draft continues.
 :changed-value                   "99.99"
 :displayed-after-external-change "bad"}
